To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries in row 1 other than $a_{14}=1$ equal to $0.$

To do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 & 4 & 1 \\ -9 & 6 & -7 & 8 \\ -9 & -4 & 9 & -4 \\ -3 & -5 & 7 & -5\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{1}:=C_{1}-4C_{4}}\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 4 & 1 \\ -41 & 6 & -7 & 8 \\ 7 & -4 & 9 & -4 \\ 17 & -5 & 7 & -5\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}+5C_{4}}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\ -41 & 46 & -7 & 8 \\ 7 & -24 & 9 & -4 \\ 17 & -30 & 7 & -5\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}-4C_{4}}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\ -41 & 46 & -39 & 8 \\ 7 & -24 & 25 & -4 \\ 17 & -30 & 27 & -5\end{bmatrix}=B
$$

Therefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are

$C_{1}:=C_{1}-4C_{4}$ and $C_{2}:=C_{2}+5C_{4}$.

To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries in row 1 other than $a_{11}=-1$ equal to $0.$

To do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 2 & 2 \\ 6 & 1 & -6 & 3 \\ 1 & 1 & -9 & -7 \\ -4 & -6 & 8 & -2\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}+5C_{1}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\ 6 & 31 & -6 & 3 \\ 1 & 6 & -9 & -7 \\ -4 & -26 & 8 & -2\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}+2C_{1}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\ 6 & 31 & 6 & 3 \\ 1 & 6 & -7 & -7 \\ -4 & -26 & 0 & -2\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{4}:=C_{4}+2C_{1}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 6 & 31 & 6 & 15 \\ 1 & 6 & -7 & -5 \\ -4 & -26 & 0 & -10\end{bmatrix}=B
$$

Therefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are

$C_{3}:=C_{3}+2C_{1}$ and $C_{4}:=C_{4}+2C_{1}$.

To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{12}=-5$ equal to $0.$

To do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:

$$
\begin{aligned}A &=\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 & -25 \\ 3 & -6 & -9 \\ 6 & -7 & 8\end{bmatrix}
\\ &\xrightarrow{C_{1}:=C_{1}+3C_{2}}\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & -25 \\ -15 & -6 & -9 \\ -15 & -7 & 8\end{bmatrix}
\\ &\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}-5C_{2}}\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 0 \\ -15 & -6 & 21 \\ -15 & -7 & 43\end{bmatrix}\\ &=B\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the elementary column operations are

$C_{1}:=C_{1}+3C_{2}$ and $C_{3}:=C_{3}-5C_{2}$.

To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of $a_{13}=-3$ equal to $0.$

To do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:

$$
\begin{aligned}A &=\begin{bmatrix}-18 & -15 & -3 \\ -1 & 2 & -6 \\ 3 & 6 & 1\end{bmatrix}
\\ &\xrightarrow{C_{1}:=C_{1}-6C_{3}}\begin{bmatrix}0 & -15 & -3 \\ 35 & 2 & -6 \\ -3 & 6 & 1\end{bmatrix}
\\ &\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}-5C_{3}}\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -3 \\ 35 & 32 & -6 \\ -3 & 1 & 1\end{bmatrix}\\ &=B\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the elementary column operations are

$C_{1}:=C_{1}-6C_{3}$ and $C_{2}:=C_{2}-5C_{3}$.
